{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# J11 — Prove bidirectional reconciliation live, against a realistic store\n\n## Why this exists\n\nEpic e369's headline AC is:\n\n> [operator-attested] rebar reconciles bidirectionally against the Dockerized Jira instance ... at\n> least one issue created in DC appears in the local store after an inbound pass, and one local edit\n> appears on the DC issue after an outbound pass, with zero unhandled exceptions\n\nIt is NOT met, and the way it failed is instructive. CI run 30637886718 reported:\n\n    invariants: scanned=0 filed=0\n    OK: steady-state pass converged\n    RECON: outbound_differ total=0   inbound_differ total=0\n\n**Scanned zero.** The pass converged because there was nothing to reconcile. That proves the\nreconciler runs cleanly against an EMPTY instance — it does not prove data moves. On an empty store\n'converged' and 'works' are indistinguishable, and only one of them is the criterion. Closing the epic\non that output would have been the fifth instance in this epic of green-but-broken.\n\nThe existing live module builds its store with the `rebar_repo` fixture: `rebar.init_repo()` on an\nempty temp dir. Nothing to sync, so nothing syncs.\n\n## Approach\n\nRun the live verification against a COPY OF THIS PROJECT'S ACTUAL TICKET STORE, so the pass sees\nrealistic volume, ticket shapes, link graphs, comment histories and field values — the conditions that\nsurface pagination, ordering and field-mapping defects an empty store cannot.\n\n### Isolation is the first requirement, not an afterthought\n\nrebar AUTO-COMMITS and AUTO-PUSHES ticket events to `sync.remote`. A copy of this project's store that\nretained its configuration could push test mutations back at the real project. Defence in depth, in\npriority order:\n\n1. **A SEPARATE REPO WITH NO REMOTES — the primary mechanism.** The CI job `git init`s a fresh repo\n   and copies the ticket-store content in. It never adds `origin`.\n   **HOW the content is obtained, because it is NOT in a normal checkout:** `.tickets-tracker/` is\n   GITIGNORED (`.gitignore:9`) and the store lives on the ORPHAN `tickets` branch, which\n   `actions/checkout` does not fetch. The job must explicitly `git fetch origin tickets` in the\n   SOURCE checkout and materialise that tree (e.g. `git archive FETCH_HEAD | tar -x -C <fresh repo>`),\n   then `git init` + commit it in the destination. Copying the workspace directory would copy nothing. There is then physically nowhere to\n   push, independent of any config or env value. This is stronger than a flag because it cannot be\n   defeated by a mis-read setting.\n2. `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=off` as a second, independent layer (`sync.push` accepts `always|async|off`;\n   the env override is authoritative).\n   **Do NOT assert on `sync.remote`.** It defaults to `\"origin\"` (`_config_schema.py:307`) whether or\n   not such a remote exists, so \"sync.remote is empty\" is neither achievable nor meaningful — and\n   setting it empty to satisfy a check would give false confidence while changing nothing. The\n   assertion that carries real weight is that `git remote` in the working repo returns NOTHING.\n3. The reconciler config points ONLY at the harness (`reconciler.base_url` = the container). No Cloud\n   credentials are present in the job environment at all — no `JIRA_URL`, no `JIRA_API_TOKEN`, no\n   `acli` auth — so the Cloud path cannot be driven even by accident.\n4. **The isolation is ASSERTED, not assumed.** A test fails the job if `git remote` returns anything in\n   the working repo, or if `sync.remote` resolves to a non-empty value. Safety that is not tested is\n   an intention.\n\n### The copy must be SCRUBBED of Jira IDs and reconciler artifacts\n\nIsolation stops ticket events ESCAPING. It does not stop the reconciler forming an intent to WRITE to\na production issue key. The scrub is an independent layer and both are required.\n\nEnumerated against this project's REAL tickets branch, not assumed:\n\n    .bridge_state/bindings.json\n    .bridge_state/bindings-retired.json\n    .bridge_state/prev_snapshot.json\n    .bridge_state.bak-retarget/bindings.json\n    .bridge_state.bak-retarget/prev_snapshot.json\n\nTwo traps a naive scrub falls into:\n\n* **`.bridge_state.bak-retarget/` also holds real bindings.** It is a backup left by a past project\n  retarget. Deleting `.bridge_state/` by name removes the live set and leaves a SECOND full set of\n  production Jira bindings in the copy. Match the pattern `.bridge_state*`, and assert none survive —\n  do not delete a hardcoded list. The existence of this directory is itself proof that the artifact\n  set drifts over time, so the scrub must ENUMERATE the copied tree.\n* **`prev_snapshot.json` is the dangerous one.** It is the previous snapshot of the REAL Cloud\n  instance. Left in place, the inbound differ diffs the DC HARNESS against production: every real\n  Cloud issue looks REMOTELY DELETED and every harness issue looks NEW. That drives the deletion path\n  across hundreds of local tickets and manufactures \"findings\" that are artifacts of the setup rather\n  than defects in the bridge. A run that produced them would be actively misleading — worse than one\n  that fails.\n\n### The DC-side mutation suite\n\nComprehensive mutations applied to the HARNESS instance (never a real Jira), each asserted to\nround-trip, so the bridge is exercised rather than merely started:\n\n* **inbound (DC -> local):** create issue; edit summary; edit description; transition status; add\n  label; remove label; add comment; edit comment; assign; unassign; create issue link; delete issue\n  link; set sub-task parent; clear parent; delete issue (absence probe).\n* **outbound (local -> DC):** the mirror set, driven by local ticket edits.\n* **volume — and note WHAT drives it.** Pagination is a property of the REMOTE issue count, not of\n  the local store copy. The store copy buys realistic local volume, ticket shapes and link graphs;\n  it does NOT exercise paging at all. To exercise paging the suite must CREATE issues in the harness.\n  Procedure, because guessing the cap is how truncation stays hidden: first READ the instance's\n  effective page size (issue a `search_issues` with a high `maxResults` and observe what the server\n  actually returns — Jira 8.17.1 defaults to 50, but `jira.search.views.default.max` is\n  server-configurable and the server silently truncates), then seed at least `2 * observed + 1`\n  issues so at least three pages are required. Assert the observed cap in the test output so a\n  future instance change is visible rather than silent.\n* **idempotence:** an immediately repeated pass after each direction writes nothing.\n\n### Sequence: a thin slice BEFORE the comprehensive suite\n\nThe riskiest unknown is not any individual mutation — it is whether the copied-and-scrubbed store\ndrives a live pass at all. So land a vertical slice first: fetch + scrub + isolation assertions, ONE\ninbound round-trip (create in DC, assert it appears locally) and ONE outbound round-trip (edit\nlocally, assert it appears in DC). Only once that is green in CI does the full mutation suite go in.\nBuilding all thirteen mutations against an unproven harness path risks debugging thirteen failures\nthat share one root cause.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `.github/workflows/external-integration.yml` — the `jira-dc-harness` job gains the store-copy step\n  (fresh `git init`, no remote) and the isolation assertions.\n- `tests/external/live_jira_dc/` — the mutation suite and its fixtures; a `dc_mutations` helper that\n  applies each DC-side change through the real transport.\n- `tests/external/live_jira_dc/README.md` — document the isolation contract and WHY (auto-push).\n\n**NOT in scope:** any change that could write to this project's Jira or its tickets branch.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] the working repo in CI has NO git remote, asserted by a test that FAILS the job otherwise —\n      this is the primary isolation and it must be verified, not assumed\n- [ ] `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=off` and an empty `sync.remote` are both asserted in the same test\n- [ ] no Cloud credential is present in the job environment, asserted by test\n- [ ] the store under test is a COPY of this project's real ticket store, and the pass reports\n      `scanned` > 0 — the specific failure that made the previous run meaningless\n- [ ] the scrub matches `.bridge_state*` as a GLOB and a test asserts ZERO binding or snapshot\n      artifacts survive anywhere in the copied tree — derived by enumerating the tree, not from a\n      hardcoded list\n- [ ] a test asserts the copied store holds NO production Jira key reference before the first pass,\n      so a missed artifact fails LOUDLY at setup instead of silently corrupting the run\n- [ ] the first inbound pass is asserted NOT to plan a mass deletion — that is the observable\n      signature of a leftover `prev_snapshot.json`, and it must fail the job rather than be read as a\n      finding\n- [ ] [operator-attested] an issue created in DC appears in the local store after an inbound pass\n- [ ] [operator-attested] a local edit appears on the DC issue after an outbound pass\n- [ ] every mutation in the suite above round-trips, each asserted individually rather than by an\n      aggregate 'converged' line\n- [ ] a repeated pass after each direction writes nothing (idempotence)\n- [ ] the effective server page size is OBSERVED and reported in the run output, not assumed, and the\n      suite seeds at least `2 * observed + 1` issues IN THE HARNESS so at least three pages are\n      required — pagination is a remote-side property and the store copy does not exercise it\n- [ ] a test asserts `git remote` in the working repo returns NOTHING. It must NOT assert on\n      `sync.remote`, which defaults to `\"origin\"` regardless of whether that remote exists\n- [ ] the store copy is obtained by explicitly fetching the orphan `tickets` branch — a normal\n      checkout does not contain it, since `.tickets-tracker/` is gitignored\n- [ ] the thin vertical slice (fetch + scrub + isolation + one inbound and one outbound round-trip)\n      is green in CI BEFORE the comprehensive mutation suite lands\n- [ ] zero unhandled exceptions, asserted on the reconciler subprocess streams\n- [ ] this project's live Jira is untouched: the suite never resolves a non-harness base URL,\n      asserted by test"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785511723643589001,"uuid":"20431a6e-ef80-43bd-8085-3757259b234c"}